WEBVTT

00:00.298 --> 00:02.024
- [Voiceover] So we have a
quadratic expression here.

00:02.024 --> 00:04.402
X squared minus three x minus 10.

00:04.402 --> 00:05.946
And what I'd like to do in this video

00:05.946 --> 00:10.070
is I'd like to factor it as
the product of two binomials.

00:10.070 --> 00:11.939
Or to put it another way,

00:11.939 --> 00:16.409
I want to write it as
the product x plus a,

00:16.409 --> 00:19.009
that's one binomial, times x plus b,

00:19.009 --> 00:23.012
where we need to figure out
what a and b are going to be.

00:23.012 --> 00:24.190
So I encourage you to pause the video

00:24.190 --> 00:26.526
and see if you can figure
out what a and b need to be.

00:26.526 --> 00:28.456
Can we rewrite this
expression as the product

00:28.456 --> 00:32.032
of two binomials where
we know what a and b are?

00:32.032 --> 00:34.592
So let's work through this together now.

00:34.592 --> 00:37.456
I'll highlight a and
b in different colors.

00:37.456 --> 00:41.885
I'll put a in yellow and
I'll put b in magenta.

00:41.885 --> 00:44.384
So one way to think about
it is let's just multiply

00:44.384 --> 00:47.594
these two binomials using a and b,

00:47.594 --> 00:49.423
and we've done this in previous videos.

00:49.423 --> 00:51.373
You might want to review
multiplying binomials

00:51.373 --> 00:53.730
if any of this looks strange to you.

00:53.730 --> 00:55.274
But if you were to multiply what we have

00:55.274 --> 00:59.378
on the right-hand side
out it would be equal to,

00:59.378 --> 01:01.247
you're going to have the x times the x

01:01.247 --> 01:03.137
which is going to be x squared.

01:03.137 --> 01:06.591
Then you're going to
have the a times the x,

01:06.591 --> 01:07.891
which is ax.

01:07.891 --> 01:09.679
And then you're going to
have the b times the x,

01:09.679 --> 01:10.553
which is bx.

01:10.553 --> 01:12.056
Actually just let me, I'm
not gonna skip any steps

01:12.056 --> 01:13.539
here just to see it this time.

01:13.539 --> 01:15.287
This is all review, or should be review.

01:15.287 --> 01:18.395
So then we have, so we did
x times x to get x squared.

01:18.395 --> 01:23.395
Then we have a times x
to get ax, to get a x.

01:24.551 --> 01:26.298
And then we're gonna have x times b,

01:26.298 --> 01:28.513
so we're multiplying each
term times every other term.

01:28.513 --> 01:31.418
So then we have x times b to get bx.

01:31.418 --> 01:36.418
So plus bx. b x.

01:37.493 --> 01:41.455
And then finally we have
plus the a times the b,

01:41.455 --> 01:46.455
which is of course going to be ab.

01:46.554 --> 01:48.058
And now we can simplify this,

01:48.058 --> 01:49.724
and you might have been
able to go straight to this

01:49.724 --> 01:52.203
if you are familiar with
multiplying binomials.

01:52.203 --> 01:54.194
This would be x squared plus,

01:54.194 --> 01:55.799
we can add these two coefficients

01:55.799 --> 01:57.261
because they're both on
the first degree terms,

01:57.261 --> 01:58.420
they're both multiplied by x.

01:58.420 --> 02:01.122
If I have ax's and I add bx's to that

02:01.122 --> 02:04.941
I'm going to have a plus b x's.

02:04.941 --> 02:06.363
So let me write that down.

02:06.363 --> 02:11.363
a plus b x's, and then
finally I have the plus,

02:14.206 --> 02:17.192
I'll do that blue color,
finally I have it.

02:17.192 --> 02:22.089
I have plus ab. Plus ab.

02:22.089 --> 02:25.908
And now we can use this to think about

02:25.908 --> 02:27.534
what a and b need to be.

02:27.534 --> 02:29.667
If we do a little bit of pattern matching,

02:29.667 --> 02:31.333
we see we have an x squared there,

02:31.333 --> 02:33.162
we have an x squared there.

02:33.162 --> 02:36.798
We have something times x,

02:36.798 --> 02:38.830
in this case it's a
negative three times x.

02:38.830 --> 02:41.654
And here we have something times x.

02:41.654 --> 02:42.853
So one way to think about it is

02:42.853 --> 02:46.273
that a plus b needs to be
equal to negative three.

02:46.273 --> 02:49.993
They need to add up to
be this coefficient.

02:49.993 --> 02:51.923
So let me write that down.

02:51.923 --> 02:56.923
So we have a plus b needs to
be equal to negative three.

03:02.874 --> 03:04.337
And we're not done yet.

03:04.337 --> 03:06.836
We finally look at this last
term, we have a times b.

03:06.836 --> 03:10.006
Well a times b needs to
be equal to negative 10.

03:10.006 --> 03:12.287
So let's write that down.

03:12.423 --> 03:17.423
So we have a times b needs
to be equal to negative 10.

03:20.388 --> 03:22.480
And in general, whenever
you're factoring something,

03:22.480 --> 03:27.295
a quadratic expression that has
a one on second degree term,

03:27.295 --> 03:29.104
so it has a one coefficient
on the x squared,

03:29.104 --> 03:30.648
you don't even see it but
it's implicitly there.

03:30.648 --> 03:32.496
You could write this as one x squared.

03:32.496 --> 03:34.447
A way to factor it is to
come up with two numbers

03:34.447 --> 03:37.941
that add up to the coefficient
on the first degree term,

03:37.941 --> 03:40.298
so two numbers that add
up to negative three.

03:40.298 --> 03:42.574
And if I multiply those same two numbers,

03:42.574 --> 03:44.910
I'm going to get negative 10.

03:44.910 --> 03:46.617
So two numbers that add
up to negative three,

03:46.617 --> 03:48.019
to add up to the coefficient here.

03:48.019 --> 03:50.477
And now when I multiply it,
I get the constant term.

03:50.477 --> 03:52.529
I get this right over here.

03:52.529 --> 03:54.683
Two numbers when I
multiply I get negative 10.

03:54.683 --> 03:57.100
Well what could those numbers be?

03:57.100 --> 04:00.270
Well since when you multiply
them we get a negative number,

04:00.270 --> 04:03.033
we know that they're going
to have different signs.

04:03.033 --> 04:05.573
And so let's see how we
could think about it.

04:05.573 --> 04:07.503
And since when we add them
we get a negative number,

04:07.503 --> 04:10.835
we know that the negative
number must be the larger one.

04:10.835 --> 04:13.781
So if I were to just factor 10,

04:13.781 --> 04:18.781
10 you could do that as one times ten,

04:22.314 --> 04:24.874
or two times five.

04:24.874 --> 04:26.479
And two and five are interesting

04:26.479 --> 04:28.226
because if one of them are negative,

04:28.226 --> 04:29.364
their difference is three.

04:29.364 --> 04:30.908
So if one is negative...

04:30.908 --> 04:32.960
So let's see if we're
talking about negative 10,

04:34.590 --> 04:38.384
you could say negative two times five.

04:38.384 --> 04:40.274
And when you multiply them
you do get negative 10.

04:40.274 --> 04:41.289
But if you add these two,

04:41.289 --> 04:42.773
you're going to get positive three.

04:42.773 --> 04:46.552
But what if you went positive
two times negative five.

04:46.552 --> 04:48.665
Now this is interesting because still

04:48.665 --> 04:51.042
when you multiply them
you get negative 10.

04:51.042 --> 04:54.800
And when you add them,
two plus negative five

04:54.800 --> 04:56.487
is going to be negative three.

04:56.487 --> 05:00.834
So we have just figured
out our two numbers.

05:00.834 --> 05:04.857
We could say that a is two or
we could say that b is two,

05:04.857 --> 05:08.616
but I'll just say that a is equal to two

05:08.616 --> 05:13.616
and b is equal to negative five.

05:14.061 --> 05:15.483
And so our original expression,

05:15.483 --> 05:16.763
we can rewrite as,

05:16.763 --> 05:20.603
so we can rewrite x squared
minus three x minus 10.

05:20.603 --> 05:22.878
We can say that that
is going to be equal to

05:22.878 --> 05:27.878
x plus two times x, instead
of saying plus negative five

05:36.491 --> 05:38.543
which we could say, we could just say,

05:38.543 --> 05:39.315
actually let me write that down.

05:39.315 --> 05:42.545
I could write just plus
negative five right over there

05:42.545 --> 05:43.399
because that's our b.

05:43.399 --> 05:48.399
I could just write x minus
five, and we're done.

05:49.189 --> 05:51.383
We've just factored it as
a product of two binomials.

05:51.383 --> 05:53.151
Now, I did it fairly involved mainly

05:53.151 --> 05:54.918
so you see where all this came from.

05:54.918 --> 05:57.011
But in the future whenever you
see a quadratic expression,

05:58.041 --> 05:59.649
and you have a one coefficient

05:59.649 --> 06:01.803
on the second degree term right over here,

06:01.803 --> 06:03.936
you could say alright well I
need to figure out two numbers

06:03.936 --> 06:06.435
that add up to the coefficient
on the first degree term,

06:06.435 --> 06:08.508
on the x term, and those same two numbers

06:08.508 --> 06:11.271
when I multiply them need to
be equal to this constant term,

06:11.271 --> 06:12.571
need to be equal to negative 10.

06:12.571 --> 06:14.257
You say okay well let's see,

06:14.257 --> 06:15.618
they're gonna be different signs

06:15.618 --> 06:17.731
because when I multiply them
I get a negative number.

06:17.731 --> 06:19.621
The negative one is
gonna be the larger one,

06:19.621 --> 06:21.917
since when I add them I
got a negative number.

06:21.917 --> 06:24.700
So let's see, let's say five
and two seem interesting.

06:24.700 --> 06:26.833
Well negative five and positive two,

06:26.833 --> 06:28.865
when you add them you're
gonna get negative three,

06:28.865 --> 06:31.208
when you multiply them
you get negative 10.
